e>Haouache</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Calvino-Gunther</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Gestin</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Armand-Lefevre</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Leflon</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaplain</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Benali</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Francais</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Adrie</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Zahar</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Thuong</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Arrault</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Croize</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Lucet</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><collab>Dressing Study Group</collab></person-group><article-title>Chlorhexidine-impregnated sponges and less frequent dressing changes for prevention of catheter-related infections in critically ill adults: a randomized controlled trial</article-title><source>JAMA</source><year>2009</year><volume>301</volume><fpage>1231</fpage><lpage>1241</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19318651</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1001/jama.2009.376</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>